-- #1111 A-2.6, the SEVERITY MIRROR.  A-2.6 turns two `exit 1`/wrong-value programs
-- into `exit 0` ones, and that direction is a severity INCREASE if it over-reaches:
-- every pre-existing fixture in this tree covered the REJECTING behaviour, so the
-- new acceptance is untested by construction and nothing derived from the diff or
-- from coverage would catch an over-widening.  This file is the deliberate opposite
-- cell — genuinely ill-typed uses of exactly the two surfaces A-2.6 touched, with
-- NO name collision anywhere in the graph, so the overlay is not even in play.
--
-- Both must still be REJECTED, with the field/argument type the ONE declaring module
-- gives them:
--   * `Cfg { fld = "s" }` — `onecfg.Cfg.fld : Int`, so `String vs Int`.
--   * `Dup "s"`           — `onecfg.Dup : Int -> A1`, so `Int vs String`.
--
-- The collision-bearing peer of the first cell (same wrong field type, but WITH
-- #1256's two same-named `Cfg`s in the graph, which must reject identically rather
-- than fall through to the other module's `Float`) is
-- `1111_a26_bystander_ctor_collision/` — that graph has the collision AND stays
-- clean, so between them the two fixtures cover "collision present, program valid"
-- and "no collision, program invalid".
import onecfg.{Cfg, A1, Dup}

bad1 : Cfg
bad1 = Cfg { fld = "s" }

bad2 : A1
bad2 = Dup "s"

main : <IO> Unit
main = println "unreachable"
